<section xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="sec-5-2-AntiderivativesFromFormulas">
  <title>Antiderivatives from Formulas</title>

  <objectives>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          If we have a function given by a formula, is there a way to write down the antiderivative?
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          How can we find the area between two curves?
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <assemblage>
      <title>Supplemental Videos</title>

      <p> The main topics of this section are also presented in the following videos: <ul>
          <li>
            <url
              href="https://unl.yuja.com/V/Video?v=7114362&amp;node=34303293&amp;a=20168581&amp;autoplay=1">Antiderivatives
        From Formulas</url>
          </li>

          <li>
            <url
              href="https://unl.yuja.com/V/Video?v=7114360&amp;node=34303269&amp;a=128183455&amp;autoplay=1">Antiderivatives
        of Other Functions</url>
          </li>

          <li>
            <url
              href="https://unl.yuja.com/V/Video?v=7114361&amp;node=34303267&amp;a=82839716&amp;autoplay=1">Using
        Antiderivatives to Compute a Definite Integral</url>
          </li>

          <li>
            <url
              href="https://unl.yuja.com/V/Video?v=7114363&amp;node=34303253&amp;a=49374719&amp;autoplay=1">Computing
        Area Between Two Curves</url>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <p> The general problem of finding an antiderivative is difficult. In part, this is
      due to the fact that we are trying to undo the process of differentiating, and the undoing is
      much more difficult than the doing. For example, while it is evident that an antiderivative of <m>f(x)
      = \sin(x)</m> is <m>F(x) = -\cos(x)</m> and that an antiderivative of <m>g(x) = x^2</m> is <m>G(x)
      = \frac{1}{3} x^3</m>, combinations of <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> can be far more complicated.
      Consider the functions <md>
        5\sin(x) - 4x^2, \ x^2 \sin(x), \ \frac{\sin(x)}{x^2}, \ \text{and} \ \sin(x^2)
      </md>.
      In the above cases, the antiderivative might not be obvious and in some cases, for example, <m>
      \sin(x^2)</m> the antiderivative cannot be written down using elementary functions. This
      warrants the question, how do we find an antiderivative from a function given by a formula in
      general? We will begin to answer that question in this chapter. </p>
  </introduction>


  <subsection xml:id="sec-4-4-ss2">
    <title>Basic antiderivatives</title>

    <p>
      What is involved in trying to find an antiderivative for each? From our experience with
      derivative rules, we know that derivatives of sums and constant multiples of basic functions
      are simple to execute, but derivatives involving products, quotients, and composites of
      familiar functions are more complicated.
      Therefore, it stands to reason that antidifferentiating products, quotients, and composites of
      basic functions may be even more challenging.
      We defer our study of all but the most elementary antiderivatives to later in the text.
    </p>

    <p>
      Before proceeding any further it will be useful to state two properties.
    </p>

    <assemblage>
      <title>Properties of Antiderivatives: Sums and Constant Multiples</title>

      <p> Let <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> be functions that have an antiderivative and <m>c</m> a constant
        then <md>
          <mrow>\int\left(f(x) \pm g(x)\right) dx &amp;= \int f(x)dx \pm \int g(x)dx</mrow>
          <mrow>\int cf(x) dx &amp;= c\int f(x)dx</mrow>
        </md>. In other words, the
        antiderivative of a sum is the sum of the antiderivatives. </p>
    </assemblage>

    <p> We forgo a formal proof of these properties but note that they are a consequence of the
      Constant Multiple Rule and Sum Rule for derivatives in <xref ref="sec-2-1-elem-rules">Section</xref>
      . </p>

    <p> We do note that whenever we know the derivative of a function, we have a <em>function-derivative
      pair</em>, so we also know the antiderivative of a function. For instance, since we know that <md>
        \frac{d}{dx}[-\cos(x)] = \sin(x)
      </md>, we also know that <m>F(x) = -\cos(x)</m>
      is an antiderivative of <m>f(x) = \sin(x)</m>. <m>F</m> and <m>f</m> together form a
      function-derivative pair. Clearly, every basic derivative rule leads us to such a pair, and
      thus to a known antiderivative. </p>

    <p> In <xref ref="act-4-4-2">Example</xref>, we will construct a list of the basic
      antiderivatives we know at this time. Those rules will help us antidifferentiate sums and
      constant multiples of basic functions. For example, since <m>-\cos(x)</m> is an antiderivative
      of <m>\sin(x)</m> and <m>\frac{1}{3}x^3</m> is an antiderivative of <m>x^2</m>, it follows
      that <md>
        F(x) = -5\cos(x) - \frac{4}{3}x^3
      </md> is an antiderivative of <m>f(x) =
      5\sin(x) - 4x^2</m>. </p>

    <p> Finally, before proceeding to build a list of common functions whose
      antiderivatives we know, we recall that each function has more than one antiderivative.
      Because the derivative of any constant is zero, we may add a constant of our choice to any
      antiderivative. For instance, we know that <m>G(x) = \frac{1}{3}x^3</m> is an antiderivative
      of <m>g(x) = x^2</m>. But we could also have chosen <m>G(x) = \frac{1}{3}x^3 + 7</m>, since in
      this case as well, <m>G'(x) = x^2</m>. If <m>g(x) = x^2</m>, we say that the <em>general
      antiderivative</em>
      <idx>
        <h>antiderivative</h>
        <h>general</h>
      </idx> of <m>g</m> is <md>
        G(x) = \frac{1}{3}x^3 + C
      </md>, where <m>C</m> represents an arbitrary real
      number constant. Regardless of the formula for <m>g</m>, including <m>+C</m> in the formula
      for its antiderivative <m>G</m> results in the most general possible antiderivative. </p>

    <p>
      In the following example, we work to build a list of basic functions whose antiderivatives we
      already know.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="act-4-4-2">
      <statement>
        <p> Use your knowledge of derivatives of basic functions to complete the above
          table of antiderivatives. For each entry, your task is to find a function <m>F</m> whose
          derivative is the given function <m>f</m>. </p>

        <table xml:id="T-4-4-Act2">
          <title>Familiar basic functions.</title>

          <tabular top="minor" bottom="minor">
            <row bottom="medium">
              <cell>given function, <m>f(x)</m></cell>
              <cell>antiderivative, <m>F(x)</m>   <nbsp />
              </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell><m>k</m>, (<m>k</m> is constant)</cell>
              <cell></cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell><m>x^n</m>, <m>n \ne -1</m></cell>
              <cell></cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>
                <m>\frac{1}{x}</m>
              </cell>
              <cell></cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>
                <m>\sin(x)</m>
              </cell>
              <cell></cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>
                <m>\cos(x)</m>
              </cell>
              <cell></cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>
                <m>\sec(x) \tan(x)</m>
              </cell>
              <cell></cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>
                <m>\csc(x) \cot(x)</m>
              </cell>
              <cell></cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>
                <m>\sec^2 (x)</m>
              </cell>
              <cell></cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>
                <m>\csc^2 (x)</m>
              </cell>
              <cell></cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>
                <m>e^x</m>
              </cell>
              <cell></cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>
                <m>a^x</m>
                <m>(a \gt 1)</m>
              </cell>
              <cell></cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>
                <m>\frac{1}{1+x^2}</m>
              </cell>
              <cell></cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>
                <m>\frac{1}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}</m>
              </cell>
              <cell></cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>
        <aside>
          <title>Antiderivative of <m>\frac{1}{x}</m></title>

          <p> The antiderivative of <m>\frac{1}{x}</m> is <m>\ln|x|+C</m>. The absolute value sign
            comes about by considering two cases separately. For <m>x>0</m>, we have that <md>
            \frac{d}{dx}\left(\ln(x)\right)=\frac{1}{x}
            </md> On the other
            hand, for <m>x&lt; 0</m>, <m>\ln|x|=\ln(-x)</m>, so <md>
            \frac{d}{dx}\left(\ln(-x)\right)=\frac{1}{-x}(-1)=\frac{1}{x}
            </md>
          </p>
        </aside>
      </statement>

      <hint>
        <p>
          You might start by constructing a list of all the basic functions whose derivative you
          know.
        </p>
      </hint>

      <answer>
        <tabular top="minor" bottom="minor">
          <row bottom="medium">
            <cell>given function, <m>f(x)</m></cell>
            <cell>antiderivative, <m>F(x)</m>   <nbsp />
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><m>k</m>, (<m>k \ne 0</m>)</cell>
            <cell>
              <m>kx+C</m>
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><m>x^n</m>, <m>n \ne -1</m></cell>
            <cell>
              <m>\frac{1}{n+1}x^{n+1}+C</m>
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>
              <m>\frac{1}{x}</m>
            </cell>
            <cell>
              <m>\ln|x|+C</m>
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>
              <m>\sin(x)</m>
            </cell>
            <cell>
              <m>-\cos(x)+C</m>
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>
              <m>\cos(x)</m>
            </cell>
            <cell>
              <m>\sin(x)+C</m>
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>
              <m>\sec(x) \tan(x)</m>
            </cell>
            <cell>
              <m>\sec(x)+C</m>
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>
              <m>\csc(x) \cot(x)</m>
            </cell>
            <cell>
              <m>-\csc(x)+C</m>
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>
              <m>\sec^2 (x)</m>
            </cell>
            <cell>
              <m>\tan(x)+C</m>
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>
              <m>\csc^2 (x)</m>
            </cell>
            <cell>
              <m>\cot(x)+C</m>
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>
              <m>e^x</m>
            </cell>
            <cell>
              <m>e^x+C</m>
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>
              <m>a^x</m>
              <m>(a \gt 1)</m>
            </cell>
            <cell>
              <m>\frac{1}{\ln(a)} a^x+C</m>
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>
              <m>\frac{1}{1+x^2}</m>
            </cell>
            <cell>
              <m>\arctan(x)+C</m>
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>
              <m>\frac{1}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}</m>
            </cell>
            <cell>
              <m>\arcsin(x)+C</m>
            </cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </answer>

      <solution>
        <table xml:id="T-4-4-Act2_2">
          <title>Familiar basic functions and their antiderivatives.</title>

          <tabular top="minor" bottom="minor">
            <row bottom="medium">
              <cell>given function, <m>f(x)</m></cell>
              <cell>antiderivative, <m>F(x)</m>   <nbsp />
              </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell><m>k</m>, (<m>k \ne 0</m>)</cell>
              <cell>
                <m>kx+C</m>
              </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell><m>x^n</m>, <m>n \ne -1</m></cell>
              <cell>
                <m>\frac{1}{n+1}x^{n+1}+C</m>
              </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>
                <m>\frac{1}{x}</m>
              </cell>
              <cell>
                <m>\ln|x|+C</m>
              </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>
                <m>\sin(x)</m>
              </cell>
              <cell>
                <m>-\cos(x)+C</m>
              </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>
                <m>\cos(x)</m>
              </cell>
              <cell>
                <m>\sin(x)+C</m>
              </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>
                <m>\sec(x) \tan(x)</m>
              </cell>
              <cell>
                <m>\sec(x)+C</m>
              </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>
                <m>\csc(x) \cot(x)</m>
              </cell>
              <cell>
                <m>-\csc(x)+C</m>
              </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>
                <m>\sec^2 (x)</m>
              </cell>
              <cell>
                <m>\tan(x)+C</m>
              </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>
                <m>\csc^2 (x)</m>
              </cell>
              <cell>
                <m>\cot(x)+C</m>
              </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>
                <m>e^x</m>
              </cell>
              <cell>
                <m>e^x+C</m>
              </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>
                <m>a^x</m>
                <m>(a \gt 1)</m>
              </cell>
              <cell>
                <m>\frac{1}{\ln(a)} a^x+C</m>
              </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>
                <m>\frac{1}{1+x^2}</m>
              </cell>
              <cell>
                <m>\arctan(x)+C</m>
              </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>
                <m>\frac{1}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}</m>
              </cell>
              <cell>
                <m>\arcsin(x)+C</m>
              </cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Evaluating Definite Integrals Involving Expressions</title>

    <p> Now that we have some basic antiderivatives in hand we can proceed to solve problems
      involving the definite integral when the integrand is an expression. Specifically, we seek to
      use antiderivatives and the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus to evaluate expressions like <m>\displaystyle
      \int_0^1 \left(x^3 - x - e^x + 2\right) \,dx</m>. </p>

    <p> Since our current interest in antiderivatives is so that we can evaluate
      definite integrals by the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus the constant <m>C</m> is actually
      irrelevant, and mathematicians genrally omit it in these types of problems. To see why,
      consider the definite integral <md>
        \int_0^1 x^2 \, dx
      </md>. </p>

    <p> For the integrand <m>g(x) = x^2</m>, suppose we find and use the general
      antiderivative <m>G(x) = \frac{1}{3} x^3 + C</m>. Then, by the Fundamental Theorem of
      Calculus, <md>
        <mrow>\int_0^1 x^2 \, dx &amp;= \left. \frac{1}{3} x^3 + C \right|_0^1</mrow>
        <mrow>&amp;= \left(\frac{1}{3} (1)^3 + C \right) - \left(\frac{1}{3} (0)^3 + C \right)</mrow>
        <mrow>&amp;= \frac{1}{3} + C - 0 - C</mrow>
        <mrow>&amp;= \frac{1}{3}</mrow>
      </md>. </p>

    <p> Observe that the <m>C</m>-values appear as opposites in the evaluation of the
      integral and thus do not affect the definite integral's value. </p>

    <example xml:id="act-5-2-3">
      <statement>
        <p> Use the FTC and the results in <xref ref="T-4-4-Act2_2">Table</xref> to
          evaluate the three given definite integrals. </p>

        <p>
          <ol marker="a.">
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>\displaystyle \int_0^1 \left(x^3 - x - e^x + 2\right) \,dx</m>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <m>\displaystyle \int_0^{\frac{\pi}{3}} (2\sin (t) - 4\cos(t) + \sec^2(t) - \pi) \,
                  dt</m>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <m>\displaystyle \int_0^1 (\sqrt{x}-x^2) \, dx</m>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>

      <hint>
        <p>
          Be sure to recall the sum and constant multiple rules, which work not only for
          differentiating, but also for antidifferentiating.
        </p>
      </hint>

      <answer>
        <p>
          <ol marker="a.">
            <li>
              <p>
                <m> \int_0^1 \left(x^3 - x - e^x + 2\right) \,dx = \frac{11}{4} - 3</m>. </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <m> \int_0^{\frac{\pi}{3}} (2\sin (t) - 4\cos(t) + \sec^2(t) - \pi) \, dt = 1 -
                \sqrt{3} - \frac{\pi^2}{3}</m>. </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <m> \int_0^1 (\sqrt{x} - x^2) \, dx = \frac{1}{3}</m>. </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </answer>

      <solution>
        <p>
          <ol marker="a.">
            <li>
              <p> By standard antiderivative rules and the FTC, <md>
                  <mrow>\int_0^1 \left(x^3 - x - e^x + 2\right) \,dx =\mathstrut \amp \left.
                \frac{1}{4} x^4 - \frac{1}{2}x^2 - e^x + 2x \right|_0^1</mrow>
                  <mrow>=\mathstrut \amp \left(\frac{1}{4} (1)^4 - \frac{1}{2}(1)^2 - e^1 + 2(1)
                \right) - \left(\frac{1}{4} (0)^4 - \frac{1}{2}(0)^2 - e^0 + 2(0) \right)</mrow>
                  <mrow>=\mathstrut \amp \frac{1}{4} - \frac{1}{2} - e + 2 - \left(0 - 0 - 1 + 0
                \right)</mrow>
                  <mrow>=\mathstrut \amp -\frac{1}{4} - e + 3</mrow>
                  <mrow>=\mathstrut \amp \frac{11}{4} - 3</mrow>
                </md>. </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p> Calculating the needed antiderivative and applying the FTC, <md>
                  <mrow>\int_0^{\frac{\pi}{3}} (2\sin (t) - 4\cos(t) + \sec^2(t) - \pi) \, dt
                =\mathstrut \amp \left. \left(-2\cos(t) - 4\sin(t) + \tan(t) - \pi t \right)
                \right|_0^{\frac{\pi}{3}}</mrow>
                  <mrow>=\mathstrut \amp \left(-2\cos(\frac{\pi}{3}) - 4\sin(\frac{\pi}{3}) +
                \tan(\frac{\pi}{3}) - \pi (\frac{\pi}{3}) \right) -</mrow>
                  <mrow>\ \amp \left(-2\cos(0)) - 4\sin(0) + \tan(0) - \pi (0) \right)</mrow>
                  <mrow>=\mathstrut \amp -2 \cdot \frac{1}{2} - 4 \cdot \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} +
                    \sqrt{3} - \frac{\pi^2}{3} - (-2 - 0 + 0 - 0)</mrow>
                  <mrow>=\mathstrut \amp 1 - \sqrt{3} - \frac{\pi^2}{3}</mrow>
                </md>. </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p> Noting that <m>\frac{d}{dx}[\frac{2}{3} x^{\frac{3}{2}}] =
                x^{\frac{1}{2}}</m>, we find that <md>
                  <mrow>\int_0^1 (\sqrt{x} - x^2) \, dx =\mathstrut \amp \left.
                \frac{2}{3}x^{\frac{3}{2}} - \frac{1}{3}x^3 \right|_0^1</mrow>
                  <mrow>=\mathstrut \amp \frac{2}{3} - \frac{1}{3} - (0 - 0 )</mrow>
                  <mrow>=\mathstrut \amp \frac{1}{3}</mrow>
                </md>. </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Integration and the Area between Two Curves</title>

    <p> Integration allows us to find the area between a curve and the <m>x</m>-axis. In fact, by
      visually inspecting the graphs of two different curves we can develop a technique for finding
      the area between two curves. Consider the graphs of the curves <m>y=x^2+1</m> and <m>y=x+3</m>
      displayed in <xref ref="F-5-2-SS4">Figure</xref>. We might ask if there is a way to find the
      area enclosed by these two curves as is colored in the figure. </p>

    <figure xml:id="F-5-2-SS4">
      <caption>Graphs of the curves <m>y=x^2+1</m> and <m>y=x+3</m>.</caption>
      <image width="50%" source="5-2-SS4.jpg" />
    </figure>

    <p>
      In fact, using the knowledge we have about integration, we can figure out this quantity
      exactly.
    </p>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p> What is the exact area enclosed by the curves given by the functions <m>f(x)=x^2+1</m>
          and <m>g(x)=x+3</m>? </p>
      </statement>

      <hint>
        <p> It might help to answer the following questions: <ul>
            <li>
              Where do the curves intersect?
            </li>

            <li> Based upon <xref ref="F-5-2-SS4">Figure</xref> how would you calculate exact area
          between <m>g(x)</m> and the <m>x</m>-axis between the points of intersection? </li>

            <li> Based upon <xref ref="F-5-2-SS4">Figure</xref> how would you calculate exact area
          between <m>f(x)</m> and the <m>x</m>-axis between the points of intersection? </li>

            <li>
              What is the relationship between the areas you calculated above?
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </hint>

      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>\frac{9}{2}</m>
        </p>
      </answer>

      <solution>
        <p> The first step in solving this problem is to determine where the curves intersect. We
          can find the point of intersection by setting the two curves equal to each other. <md>
            <mrow>x^2+1 \amp=x+3</mrow>
            <mrow>x^2-x-2 \amp=x+3</mrow>
            <mrow>x^2-x-2 \amp=0</mrow>
            <mrow>(x+1)(x-2) \amp=0</mrow>
          </md> Therefore, the curves intersect at two <m>
          x</m>-values, <m>x=-1,2</m>. This means were are concerned with the interval <m>[-2,2]</m>
          . </p>

        <p> We next note that on the interval in question, <m>[-2,2]</m>, the curve <m>g(x)=x+3</m>
          is above the curve <m>f(x)=x^2+1</m>. We also note that <m>\int_{-1}^{2}(x+3)dx</m> would
          give the area shown in <xref ref="F-5-2-SS5">Figure</xref> and <m>\int_{-1}^{2}(x^2+1)dx</m> <xref
            ref="F-5-2-SS6">Figure</xref>. </p>

        <figure xml:id="F-5-2-SS5">
          <caption>Graph of the curve <m>y=x+3</m>.</caption>
          <image width="25%" source="5-2-SS5.jpg" />
        </figure>

        <figure xml:id="F-5-2-SS6">
          <caption>Graph of the curve <m>y=x+3</m>.</caption>
          <image width="25%" source="5-2-SS6.jpg" />
        </figure>

        <p> By overlaying <xref ref="F-5-2-SS5">Figure</xref> and <xref ref="F-5-2-SS6">Figure</xref>
          as in <xref ref="F-5-2-SS7">Figure</xref> you can see that <xref ref="F-5-2-SS4">Figure</xref>
          should be given by subtracting <m>\int_{-1}^{2}(x^2+1)dx</m> from <m>\int_{-1}^{2}(x+3)dx</m>
          . </p>

        <figure xml:id="F-5-2-SS7">
          <caption>Graphs of the curves <m>y=x^2+1</m> and <m>y=x+3</m>.</caption>
          <image width="25%" source="5-2-SS7.jpg" />
        </figure>

        <p> Therefore, the area between the curves <m>y=x^2+1</m> and <m>y=x+3</m> is given by the
          following quantity: <md>
            <mrow> \int_{-1}^{2}(x+3)\,dx-\int_{-1}^{2}(x^2+1)\,dx \amp=
          \int_{-1}^{2}\left((x+3)-(x^2+1)\right)\,dx </mrow>
            <mrow> \mathstrut \amp=\int_{-1}^{2}(-x^2+x+2)\,dx</mrow>
            <mrow> \mathstrut \amp= \left. -\frac{1}{3}x^3+\frac{1}{2}x^2+2x \right|_{-1}^{2}</mrow>
            <!--
                    <mrow>\mathstrut \amp=
            -\frac{1}{3}(2)^3+\frac{1}{2}(2)^2+2(2)-\left(-\frac{1}{3}(-1)^3+\frac{1}{2}(-1)^2+2(-1)\right)</mrow>
                    <mrow>\mathstrut \amp =-\frac{8}{3}+2+4-\left(\frac{1}{3}+\frac{1}{2}-2\right)</mrow>
                    -->
            <mrow>\mathstrut \amp= \frac{9}{2}</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>

